ial center  since the austro-hungarian monarchy  that's the 19th century  this brought in a lot of workers the  industries  mostly germans and in the beginning i  mentioned that it was called aussie  and that's because it was basically a  german city up to the second world war  in the 1930 census the city had 43  793 residents of which  32 878 were german 8735 or czech or  slovak  222 jews 16 russians and 11  fellow compatriot hungarians in 1938 of  course the city was taken over by the  nazis  and towards the end of world war ii the  city was taken back by advancing soviet  forces  but the point where the city changed  forever basically  was the summer of 1945 where the  so-called usti massacre happened when an  ammo depot exploded killing around 27  people  seven of whom were czechs and this  somehow triggered a massive lynching of  germans in the city  the official number of victims ranged  from 80 to 120  but some estimates go all the way to 4  000 which is most likely overshooting it  the psychosis was so incredible that at  one point a mother and her baby  were thrown into the albert from a  bridge and then shot at by soldiers  and after the revolution it turned out  that this whole thing was actually  orchestrated by the czechoslovakian  secret service to make a better case for  the explosion of germans  into germany in which they succeeded and  the vast majority of the city's original  population was just basically uh  deported  so here we are after the war in the 50s  60s  the industry is being rebuilt new  residential complexes are being erected  and new people are being shipped in the  war didn't damage the city as much  the overwhelming majority of the city  fabric is still undamaged  so what was the czechoslovakian  leadership's idea for urban planning  they wanted to turn using alabama into a  model city  with all the latest innovations in  planning and shine new architecture  in practice this mostly meant styles  such as soviet modernism and brutalism  and of course your average blocks of  flats the plan was to move people  and mass to large residential areas  around the center  and turn the center itself into an  exclusively governmental slash  commercial district  with brand new architecture the only  problem was that the city was already  there  and in such cases you can't really make  big changes and you shouldn't either  unless it's absolutely necessary so the  czechoslovakian government said  okay and then they just started blasting  entire blocks were just completely  leveled  this meant blowing up multiple dozens of  buildings  including most of the main street only  to be built back up with grey blocks  and turns out these architectural styles  don't really age too well especially if  they aren't maintained  so half the main street was essentially  leveled which meant blowing up  19 apartment buildings simultaneously  which is an all-time record in a country  for this they had to custom design a  demolition switch that had twice the  capacity of the best devices at the time  able to detonate 20 000 demolition  charges at the same time  so in 1980 half the main street was  blown up but the leadership was just  getting started the strikov district in  particular  an entire neighborhood called kremlin  was leveled the plots of which have not  been built up to this day  now there's a couple of giant open  fields  instead of you know housing for people  according to the original plans  in the city center only the church and a  bank building were to remain  that's two buildings in total everything  else was planned to be demolished  eventually  but thankfully most of these plans could  not be realized due to a  lack of money this is how the main  street's second half was saved by the  way they just ran out of money  that being said large-scale demolitions  went on all the way until 1989  the revolution and in some cases  continue to this day  all the way in a smaller scale  thankfully for example next to the 