ive when an area is at risk of  becoming a ghetto  uh what you do there is uh launch social  programs uh make sure kids attend school  properly you help train people for  getting new jobs maybe even buy out some  of their homes and help them relocate  or you know increase police presence in  the area but since the affected people  were roma  of course none of this happened and  instead their neighborhoods were just  basically left to rot with all kinds of  social problems very quickly spiraling  out of control  so when problems became unbearable in  one particular neighborhood  the city decided it's time to do  something finally in the form of  building a  two meter tall 150 meter long wall  to separate roma residents from non-roma  and this is the equivalent of me coming  home and  noticing that the bedroom is on fire so  i just lock its door and go to sleep in  the living room  the offense stood for a grand total of  five weeks after which it was torn down  due to public pressure  uh even vaslav pavel himself came to  usti to handle the situation  after this fiasco the city was given  some grant money to help solve the  social problems  but they went ahead and used this money  to buy out the apartments of the  non-roma in the area  and essentially evacuating them out thus  creating aroma only ghetto  so their strategy is basically to ignore  social problems and let them run for a  while and then just  [ __ ] about it so let's check with  slovakian urban planning for you  what really astounded me during my  research for this video is the almost  sociopathic  negligence of the authorities both for  the built environment and for the people  and honestly i don't think i've ever  seen destruction of historical buildings  on this scale outside the u.s  but hopefully ustin alabama will become  a lesson for future urban planners  one can only hope anyway thanks for  watching and if you have a historic city  center  please don't blow it up  [Music]  you

